{
  "id": "Compound::DB00358 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Mefloquine is linked to Cyp3a4 through a series of molecular interactions that involve the enzyme CYP3A4. The evidence suggests that Mefloquine exerts its effects by causing respiratory disorders, which in turn are associated with the binding of specific compounds to Cyp3a4. For instance, Mefloquine causes respiratory disorders that are triggered by Chloramphenicol or Maraviroc, both of which bind to Cyp3a4. This indicates that Mefloquine may indirectly influence Cyp3a4 activity through the activation of downstream pathways involving these compounds. The shared role of Cyp3a4 in metabolizing these substances suggests a potential mechanism by which Mefloquine could modulate Cyp3a4 function, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of Cyp3a4 in drug metabolism and its potential involvement in the pharmacological effects of Mefloquine.",
